The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
len = a->len-1;
for (i=0; i<len; i++) {
```
Reason:
The calculation of `len` as `a->len - 1` incorrectly reduces the length of the garbage collection root array, which can lead to partial copies of objects during the `memmove` operation. This results in improper handling of unregistered objects, as the loop iterates one less than the actual length of the array, potentially skipping the last element and causing inconsistencies.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < a->len; i++) {
    memmove(&a->ptr[i], &a->ptr[i + 1], (a->len - i) * sizeof(a->ptr[i]));
    a->len--;
```
Reason:
The fix removes the incorrect `len` calculation and uses the actual length of the array `a->len` in the loop condition. Additionally, it correctly calculates the size of the memory to be moved by multiplying the number of elements to shift by `sizeof(a->ptr[i])`, ensuring that the entire object is properly handled during the unregistration process. This restores the expected behavior of allowing unregistered objects to be used as arguments.

